===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|trig|lang=enm}}, {{term|tryg|lang=enm}}, {{etyl|non|en}} {{term|tryggr||loyal, faithful, true|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*triwwiz||loyal, faithful, true}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|trēowe||faithful, loyal, true}}. More at {{l|en|true}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|trigger}}

# {{context|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal|lang=en}} [[true|True]]; [[trusty]]; [[trustworthy]]; [[faithful]].
# {{context|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal|lang=en}} [[safe|Safe]]; [[secure]].
# {{context|now|_|chiefly|_|dialectal|lang=en}} [[tight|Tight]]; [[firm]]; [[steady]]; [[sound]]; in good condition or health.
# [[neat|Neat]]; [[tidy]]; [[trim]]; [[spruce]]; [[smart]].
#* Brit. Quart. Rev.
#*: To sit on a horse square and '''trig'''.
#* '''1973''', ''{{w|Newsweek}}'' 1973, April 16th <!-- Cited as a late example of usage in a major magazine -->
#*:The [torture] stories seemed incongruent with the men telling them – a trim, '''trig''' lot who, given a few pounds more flesh, might have stepped right out of a recruiting poster.
